Since the given function is a rational function, it is a proper rational function. So by Theorem 1.3.10, the given function has vertical asymptote at $2$ and $-2$, which implies that $q(x)$ has roots $(x - 2)(x + 2) = x^2 - 4$. Since $\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 5x + 3}{q_x}$ has no horizontal asymptotes, and the degree of the numerator is 3, the degree of $q(x)$ has to be $2$. So we can express $q(x)$ as \begin{align*}q(x) = ax^2 + bx + c.\end{align*}</think> <answer>The system of equations ($q(2) = 0$ and $q(-2) = 0$) gives us \begin{align*}\begin{cases}4a + 2b + c = 0\\ 4a - 2b + c = 0 \end{cases}\end{align*}
From the last equation, we get $c = -4a$.
Substituting $c = -4a$, we have \begin{align*}\begin{cases}4a + 2b + c = 0\\ 4a - 2b + c = 0 \end{cases} \Longrightarrow \begin{cases}4a + 2b + c = 0\\ 4a - 2b - 4a = 0 \end{cases} \Longrightarrow \begin{cases}4a + 2b + c = 0\\ 2b = 0 \end{cases}\end{align*}
Since $b = 0$, we have $c = -4a$. The last equation gives us the following: $$\begin{cases}4a + 2b + c = 0\\ 2b = 0 \end{cases}$$ which reduces to \begin{align*}\begin{cases} c = -4a \\ 2b = 0 \end{cases} \end{align*} Since $2b = 0$, we have $b = 0$. From $c = -4a$ and $b = 0$, we have that $q(x) = ax^2 - 4a$. Since $a \neq 0$, we have $a(x^2 - 4)$. Note that we can factor out $a$ from $a(x^2) - 4a = a(x^2 - 4)$ and $a$ is a nonzero number. Therefore, we have \begin{align*}q(x) = a(x^2 - 4)\end{align*}
Substituting $x = 3$ to $q(x) = a(x^2 - 4)$ gives us \begin{align*}q(3) = a(3^2 - 4) & = 15\\ a(5) & = 15\\ a & = 3\end{align*}
So, $q(x) = 3(x^2 - 4)$, which can also be expressed as $q(x) = 3x^2 - 12$.
Thus, the answer is $\boxed{3x^2 - 12}$.</answer>

